          "id": "beeee4b9385d2bc9ec59f7c170a73a4afb558203",
          "message": "fix: avoid detoasting mutable segments during VACUUM (#5365) (#5402)\n\n# Ticket(s) Closed\n\n- Closes #5365\n\n## What\n\n`ambulkdelete` no longer detoasts heap rows when processing a\n**mutable**\n(in-memory) segment during VACUUM. It now reads that segment's live\nctids\ndirectly from the segment's own add/remove log instead of from the\nTantivy\nctid fast field.\n\n## Why\n\nVACUUM only ever needs each segment's ctids to drive the bulk-delete\ncallback.\nFor a mutable segment, reading them via\n`FFType::new_ctid(segment_reader.fast_fields())`\nlazily **materializes** the segment, which re-fetches and\n`pg_detoast_datum`s its\nheap rows. Concurrent with a VACUUM freeing those same TOAST chunks, the\ndetoast\nraises spurious TOAST-corruption errors even though the table is\nphysically intact:\n\n```\nERROR: missing chunk number 0 for toast value ... in pg_toast_...\nERROR: unexpected chunk number 196 (expected 0) for toast value ... in pg_toast_...\n```\n\nThis is the VACUUM-side code path called out in #5365 (distinct from the\nquery-side materialization path). As the issue notes, VACUUM should\nnever\nreconstruct full documents or detoast arbitrary user columns from the\nheap.\n\n## How\n\nIn the `ambulkdelete` per-segment loop, branch on\n`directory.is_mutable(&segment_id)`:\n\n- **Mutable segments** — read live ctids from\n`SegmentMetaEntry::mutable_snapshot`,\nwhich reconstructs the set of live ctids purely from the segment's\nin-index\nadd/remove log. No heap fetch, no detoast. Deletes are applied through\nthe\nexisting `SegmentDeleter::Mutable` path, which is already keyed by ctid\n(the\n  `doc_id` argument is unused for mutable segments).\n- **Persisted segments** — unchanged: keep reading the ctid fast field\nby\n  `doc_id`. This path does not detoast.\n\nThis is safe because materialization is fully lazy: in our Tantivy fork\n`SegmentReader::open` reads no files (all component readers are\n`OnceLock`-backed),\nand only `fast_fields()` triggers the directory's mutable-segment\nreconstruction.\nOpening the VACUUM reader therefore never materializes anything; the\nchange is\ncontained entirely to the loop body. The `vacuum_delay_point` interrupt\ncheck is\nfactored into a small closure shared by both branches.\n\n`mutable_snapshot` yields exactly the same ctid set the materialized\nsegment's\nctid fast field would have (the materialization builds documents from\nthat same\nsnapshot), so delete behavior is unchanged.\n\n## Tests\n\n- New `pg_regress` test `mutable-toast-vacuum`: builds a BM25 index with\nmutable +\nlarge toasted rows, deletes/updates rows to leave dead heap tuples, runs\n`VACUUM`,\nand asserts it completes without error and reclaims exactly the dead\nrows.\n- Existing `mutable-toast`, `heap_filter_vacuum`, and `parallel_vacuum`\nregress\n  tests pass unchanged.\n\n> Note: this fixes the VACUUM-side path. The deterministic concurrency\n> reproduction (`toast-mutable-race-local.toml` from the issue)\nexercises the race\n> directly; the regress test added here is a single-backend correctness\nguard.\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.8 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nCo-authored-by: Mithun Chicklore Yogendra <mithun.cy@gmail.com>",
